Here are all **six launch files** in your project, plus SLAM and standalone RViz commands.

## 1. Robot Model In RViz Only

On the laptop, with no Gazebo or hardware:

```bash
cd ~/ros2_Nav2_diffbot
source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
source install/setup.bash

ros2 launch my_robot_description display.launch.py
```

The XML version does the same job:

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_description display.launch.xml
```

Both open RViz and the joint-control GUI.

## 2. Gazebo Simulation

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup my_robot_gazebo.launch.xml
```

This starts Gazebo, the simulated robot, and RViz.

Without the Gazebo graphical window:

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup my_robot_gazebo.launch.xml gui:=false
```

## 3. Simulation Navigation

With Gazebo already running and SLAM stopped:

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup navigation.launch.py \
  map:=$HOME/my_maps/my_world.yaml \
  use_sim_time:=true
```

Change the map path to your saved simulation map. This launch starts Nav2, not Gazebo.

## 4. Physical Robot Bringup

On the **Pi**, source Jazzy and your workspace:

```bash
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source ~/ros2_Nav2_diffbot/install/setup.bash

ARDUINO=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Arduino__www.arduino.cc__0042_855313030313514001C1-if00
LIDAR=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Silicon_Labs_CP2102_USB_to_UART_Bridge_Controller_0001-if00-port0
```

Start the Mega, controllers, robot description, and LiDAR:

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup physical_bringup.launch.py \
  serial_port:=$ARDUINO lidar_serial_port:=$LIDAR \
  use_rviz:=false
```

For a **wheel test without LiDAR**:

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup physical_bringup.launch.py \
  serial_port:=$ARDUINO use_lidar:=false use_rviz:=false
```

Standalone bringup accepts manual commands on `/cmd_vel`; those commands are **not collision-monitor protected**.

## 5. Physical Navigation With Saved Map

This launch starts **both physical bringup and Nav2**. Don’t run a second physical bringup alongside it.

```bash
ros2 launch my_robot_bringup physical_navigation.launch.py \
  map:=$HOME/my_maps/physical_map.yaml \
  serial_port:=$ARDUINO lidar_serial_port:=$LIDAR \
  use_rviz:=false
```

Change the map path to your real saved map. **Do not send a goal yet**: first complete the checks in [PHYSICAL_NAVIGATION.md](/home/ragnarhritz/ros2_Nav2_diffbot/docs/PHYSICAL_NAVIGATION.md), including verifying the collision monitor, localization, and physical stopping behaviour.

## 6. SLAM Mapping

Run in another terminal with either Gazebo or physical bringup already running:

```bash
# Physical robot
ros2 launch slam_toolbox online_async_launch.py use_sim_time:=false
```

```bash
# Gazebo simulation
ros2 launch slam_toolbox online_async_launch.py use_sim_time:=true
```

Stop SLAM before starting saved-map navigation with AMCL.

## 7. RViz On Laptop Docker

For visualization of the running physical robot:

```bash
sudo docker start diffbot_jazzy
sudo docker exec -it diffbot_jazzy bash
rviz2 -d /data/diffbot.rviz
```

Use `odom` as the fixed frame for a hardware-only test, or `map` when SLAM/AMCL is running.


## 8. LAunching SLam
There isn’t a combined mapping launch file in your project yet. Start SLAM **separately**, after physical bringup:

```bash
ros2 launch slam_toolbox online_async_launch.py use_sim_time:=false
```

For Gazebo, use `use_sim_time:=true`. Don’t run saved-map navigation/AMCL at the same time.

## 9. TO save mapped layout

While SLAM is running:

```bash
cd ~/my_maps
ros2 run nav2_map_server map_saver_cli -f ~/my_maps/physical_map
```

This saves `physical_map.yaml` and `physical_map.pgm` in `~/my_maps`.

## To tun Teleop
With **physical bringup and SLAM already running**, open a new Pi terminal for teleop:

```bash
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
source ~/ros2_Nav2_diffbot/install/setup.bash

ros2 run teleop_twist_keyboard teleop_twist_keyboard \
  --ros-args -p stamped:=false -p speed:=0.05 -p turn:=0.3
```

Use `i` forward, `,` backward, `j/l` turn, and `k` stop. Drive slowly and keep the robot in sight. Manual teleop is **not collision-monitor protected**.

When mapping is complete, open another terminal **while SLAM is still running**:

```bash
source /opt/ros/jazzy/setup.bash
mkdir -p ~/my_maps

ros2 run nav2_map_server map_saver_cli -f ~/my_maps/physical_map \
  --ros-args -p map_subscribe_transient_local:=true
```

Your map will be saved as `~/my_maps/physical_map.yaml` and `physical_map.pgm`.